      {
        "id": 118022,
        "name": "Reckless",
        "username": "Reckless",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/reckless/{size}/2467_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-12-06T15:35:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>The frustration I have is when the conversation goes something like this:</p>\n<p>“I love using these specific cards with Fixer against this specific villain.”</p>\n<p>“That does sound like fun. I wanted to do that and every time I’ve played Fixer against that villain I haven’t had the opportunity. I’ve often had trouble getting the fun combos with him.”</p>\n<p>“The thing about Fixer is playing his hand is better than playing his deck.”</p>\n<p>I’m fine with either argument. Put them together and it sounds like the problem was that the player that didn’t get the combo (mutually agreed upon as useful and fun) is somehow in the wrong or ignorant, rather than just missing out on the fun.</p>\n<p>Then it ends with “Fixer may not be the character for you.” That is also disappointing. The players both acknowledge the fun that could be had, but the player who didn’t have a good experience (an experience brought up by the player who had a good time) gets put in the same category as people who claim Fixer is underpowered or boring.</p>\n<p>It feels like the player is the problem for not having the fun that he or she should have had, rather than a history of poor hands or bad games.</p>",
        "post_number": 122,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:50:50.465Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
